#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172853} :activity/id #uuid "deaf4c92-c8a0-5ffb-ac01-faeb66f39a39" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224 :topic/id #uuid "89b54209-d10d-5a8f-8829-a2f0c5ee66d4" :topic/math-academy-id 3353} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173121 :tutorial/content "A **Poisson process** is a mathematical model used to describe events occurring randomly over continuous space or time. A Poisson process is characterized by the following properties:\n\n- Events occur *independently*. In other words, one event's occurrence does not affect another event's likelihood.\n\n- Events occur at a *constant average rate*. The rate at which events occur represents the expected number of events per unit of time or space.\n\nWe encountered the exponential distribution in a previous lesson. The exponential distribution models the amount of continuous space or time $X$ between two events in a Poisson process with success rate $\\lambda.$ \n\nWe have already seen that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ follows an exponential distribution, then the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/72/72cd99ec8ff51659357f00417203ace84164027e0cb88584cc136f608d86b15a.svg)\n\nThe exponential distribution is often considered the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. Recall that the geometric distribution models the number of trials before the first success in a sequence of independent Bernoulli trials.\n\nFor example, suppose a call center receives telephone calls at an average rate of $3$ calls per minute. If the random variable $X$ represents the amount of time between two consecutive calls, then $X$ can be modeled using an exponential distribution, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the time between two consecutive calls is less than one minute is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-3x}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-3}]-[-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-3}]+[1] \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.9502.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. If the call center receives calls at an average rate of $3$ per minute, then the probability of waiting less than one minute until the next call is received should be relatively large." :tutorial/id #uuid "3c557f1b-8f84-52d6-823a-8025e70b3114" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10786 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "86f3cc3d-b825-558e-89db-f7ded0fe1e84" :step/math-academy-id 231085 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171794 :question/id #uuid "fa51ca96-4ed2-585b-88e6-244b4d8dd66d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050018}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435504}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "b8119b3b-4fff-596e-94aa-acf0dc17ccff" :step/math-academy-id 231086 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171795 :question/id #uuid "4c677102-9f65-58f2-8998-8353f81d84d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb820969-b00e-58fd-9e6d-f8c786f91b33" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544894}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Bounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "3ef50142-79f2-5496-a8b7-805b3150108d" :step/math-academy-id 231089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172007 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171796 :question/id #uuid "4baa7bca-acfb-5f9b-a6b4-1fc4b949555d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16590"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "faf45d2f-484a-56ee-af3e-c14a63721c19" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544872}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Unbounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "5e0e069a-0219-5cb2-bbfa-c62742dcfbf9" :step/math-academy-id 231092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173122 :tutorial/content "The memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that the probability of an event occurring in the future is independent of the time already passed. \n\nFor a random variable $X$, the probability that the event occurs in the next $t$ units of time, given it hasn't occurred after $s$ units of elapsed time, is\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSuppose you have a radioactive particle whose decay time $X$ follows the exponential distribution with rate $\\lambda = 0.8 \\text{ decays/minute}.$\n\nThen, the probability that the particle will decay in the first minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0.8{e}^{-0.8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-0.8x}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.8}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-0.8} \\\\  & ≈0.5507.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose the particle doesn't decay in the first minute. What is the probability that it will decay in the next minute? We can answer this question using the memoryless property.\n\nBy the memoryless property, we have\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nTaking the complement of this event, we have\n\n$$\nP(X < s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X < t) = 1-e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $s=1$ and $t=1,$ so\n\n$$\nP(X < 1+1 \\, \\big|\\, X>1) = P(X < 1) \\approx 0.5507\n$$\n\nIn other words, even if the particle has survived for one minute, the probability that it will decay in the next minute remains the same as at the start of the observations." :tutorial/id #uuid "50a52f5a-aa3a-5935-9354-a35e097ff394" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10787 :tutorial/title "Modeling Using the Memoryless Property"} :step/id #uuid "48fbdce6-fb42-59ad-9328-65fe0616ba01" :step/math-academy-id 231095 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172858}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172008 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171797 :question/id #uuid "821b7a41-c7ee-54fc-9b12-b13330b109ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-16591"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e40fd627-b52a-5d60-b144-2087979533fe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544895}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"} :step/id #uuid "a11e0baa-9867-5238-a3a8-1e70022bc1f2" :step/math-academy-id 231096}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172175}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485624} :activity/id #uuid "616b3a6a-2237-5567-8bd3-bad7d041996f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224 :topic/id #uuid "89b54209-d10d-5a8f-8829-a2f0c5ee66d4" :topic/math-academy-id 3353} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb7dd9eb-cdeb-501a-a394-258b4c0a70a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171448} {:answer/id #uuid "1b166edc-f9ee-54cf-a9dd-16b3169ff4a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0111" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171449} {:answer/id #uuid "1e94cdee-8432-5001-bc67-ae18a90f7237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0498" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450} {:answer/id #uuid "268664a8-6e49-52a3-8c87-9405b615b645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171451} {:answer/id #uuid "339dc04c-c725-5bea-bd34-5df67317f404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7165" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171452}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450} :answer-field/id #uuid "02f76e3c-1245-5939-a1bc-92a2d23477ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ec6131e7-de2b-527c-b4ba-ef95dae870b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-96381" :question/problem "At a call center, the time taken for phone calls to be answered varies continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $3$ calls every minute. What is the probability of taking more than $1$ minute to answer the next call? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time until the next call is answered occurs continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, between calls. Then, since there are $3$ calls per minute on average, the rate is $\\lambda =3$ calls per minute.\n\nThe amount of time before the next call can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =3,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next call will occur after $1$ minute or more is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.0498.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "362e8628-dbd8-5502-bb8d-0fc43dd4f500" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87cb0385-7440-5272-98a7-928a22fd8042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485112} :answer-field/id #uuid "95f02d2d-c10d-5ca4-b6d9-0139b28173a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "13a56b51-6b01-5125-b67e-75c6aab58d86" :question/math-academy-id "q-253892" :question/problem "At a particular helpdesk, customer waiting times occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $6$ customers per hour. If a customer has already waited for $6$ minutes, what is the probability that they will need to wait at least $4$ more minutes for their call to be answered? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since waiting times occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the customer's waiting time, in minutes. Since the help desk serves $6$ customers per hour on average (or $1$ customer every $10$ minutes), the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ customers per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ $s=6,$ and $t=4.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6+4\\,|\\,X>6) & =P(X>4) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/10)\\cdot (4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2/5} \\\\  & ≈0.670.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48f1bc6d-2333-56a9-97a8-8077e356fb41" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e33eb3c-cec7-5493-9f32-c984010c4bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0302" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484871} {:answer/id #uuid "275b76d2-5fd6-553c-b3f2-cd785fa8f998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484889} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7f8381-9c80-59cc-9eb2-c3583a9235be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2485" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485128} {:answer/id #uuid "c29e1a84-9ffd-505b-8a79-6dcd87d514f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4353" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485233} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ea36b6-b0d0-5067-953e-f4ef81d92a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7515" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485128} :answer-field/id #uuid "a99fb244-28cb-58fa-857b-0ab86cacbcc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99aa1c5a-eed9-5ae6-b8c6-e2b13db7c0f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96367" :question/problem "Mark runs a hotel near a popular tourist resort. He knows that, on average, people usually book their room $3.5$ days before their trip. By modeling the number of days between the customer's booking and arrival as continuous and assuming that these numbers are independent, determine the probability that the next customer books their room less than $1$ full day in advance. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time interval between the customer's booking and arrival is continuous and the time of different bookings are independent and occur at a constant average rate, we can model the time between the next customer's booking and arrival using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of days between a booking and the customer's arrival. Since customers book their room $3.5=7/2$ days in advance on average, $X$ can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/7,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{2}{7}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-2x/7}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer books a room less than $1$ full day in advance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-2x/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x/7}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/7}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.2485\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "81c056b4-eadd-5982-a819-4d1188c88ca4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5baebaba-fb25-532c-a3b0-5a6c14ea2ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.264" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485020}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485020} :answer-field/id #uuid "938386cd-7f18-57c1-8aef-c0067eb1a186" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b588bb0d-6d8e-5a2d-ae2c-642f8c01ed06" :question/math-academy-id "q-254586" :question/problem "A fisherman catches fish continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $1$ fish every $15$ minutes. If the fisherman has already been fishing for $30$ minutes without catching any fish, what is the probability that he will need to fish for at least $20$ more minutes before catching his first fish? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since catching fish occurs continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next catch using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next catch, in minutes. Since the fisherman catches $1$ fish every $15$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15}$ fish per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{15}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15},$ $s=30,$ and $t=20.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30+20\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X>20) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/15)\\cdot (20)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4/3} \\\\  & ≈0.264.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9424cb41-3dfd-528c-948b-f29f36193749"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c54cf95-8d93-5f06-addc-038acebc5019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2592" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484841} {:answer/id #uuid "0c65b508-2845-571b-8454-76c6030fae62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2952" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484842} {:answer/id #uuid "48a6a32b-b9ab-5bec-85a1-8073a3608631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2529" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484974} {:answer/id #uuid "5814f8c1-9cae-53cc-8145-d58f8cc938cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2552" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485016} {:answer/id #uuid "668716e1-89c4-559b-bcb0-1a077d43cacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2599" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485041}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484841} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2f20a1-a54d-5ed7-a2a8-fb23d83de889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b831793d-1ec0-54c8-869c-6f86de445418" :question/math-academy-id "q-132546" :question/problem "Vehicles arrive at a crossing continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ seconds. Determine the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds. Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time intervals between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing are continuous and independent, and arrivals occur at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next vehicle arrives as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing. Then, since vehicles arrive every $10$ seconds on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/10$ vehicles per second.\n\nThe time between arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =1/10,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/10}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3/10}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3/10} \\\\  & ≈0.2592\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ce0bc65b-a275-58a3-b6a0-d989f4a9ad43" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485616}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484806}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544983} :activity/id #uuid "281bfa6d-174e-50b3-ac57-05c41fca1504" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224 :topic/id #uuid "89b54209-d10d-5a8f-8829-a2f0c5ee66d4" :topic/math-academy-id 3353} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f15c2a-e5fc-5ca0-bb85-07dbfb14f2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.487" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544422} :answer-field/id #uuid "d936cedc-6149-5dd3-984d-e6f7a43f2a04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1e2b523-bf44-5c32-9b95-836b1e8d2e60" :question/math-academy-id "q-254609" :question/problem "While riding on a road, a cyclist encounters potholes continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $4$ potholes per hour. If the cyclist has not encountered any potholes in the past $30$ minutes, what is the probability that he will encounter a pothole in the next $10$ minutes? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since the cyclist encounters potholes continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next pothole as an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, until the cyclist encounters the next pothole. Since the cyclist encounters, on average, $4$ potholes per hour (or $1$ pothole every $15$ minutes), the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15}$ potholes per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{15}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. Therefore, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15},$ $s=30,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 30+10\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X\\le 10) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(1/15)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.487.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0cfd11fc-dd9a-5b3c-8a96-28e4560b2041"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caa8381d-0308-5944-b790-50b787cd73b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544516} :answer-field/id #uuid "63853831-cd8f-5815-844b-593361f11ad4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d44e0172-2699-5b95-8b6b-c9e2c6a8cdf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-254581" :question/problem "A customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes. If a ticket has been open for $20$ minutes already, what is the probability that it will remain unresolved for at least another $10$ minutes? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since the customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next ticket is resolved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the ticket resolving time, in minutes. Since the team resolves $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}$ tickets per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{5}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5},$ $s=20,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>20+10\\,|\\,X>20) & =P(X>10) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/5)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.135.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33983c3f-9e33-5e47-b127-51f52627feeb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596675b4-21d2-5ac4-b0a2-4839e83555d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8647" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544284} {:answer/id #uuid "5ccd3213-2b5a-598b-9a24-9bb6f2190670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8785" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544289} {:answer/id #uuid "d687dca3-0bd3-526a-a4ab-509c47139640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544547} {:answer/id #uuid "daa90ef2-43bc-5481-8ed8-ea54977434e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8892" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544558} {:answer/id #uuid "e7be2a9b-7b4a-533e-9a2f-8958ec2a1213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7923" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4dfd42d-e350-5855-be90-2f0889abb592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eddfe563-295a-5298-9f24-7cdb158be9e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132486" :question/problem "A monitor issues a warning signal when an action is needed as part of a production process. Signals occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $0.2$ minutes.  Determine the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the monitor issues a warning signal continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next signal appears using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between successive signals. Since the monitor issues a warning signal every $0.2=\\frac{1}{5}$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =5$ signals per minute.\n\nThe times between two successive signals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(5).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-5x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}5{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-5x}|}_{0}^{0.4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "979ed7bb-94cd-5512-96ff-35ef9c614946" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac5709fe-0354-5d44-b05a-02a21b0da97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544456} {:answer/id #uuid "d25214fe-4395-53a2-b548-fff04525e163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544534} {:answer/id #uuid "dbff2e28-f33a-5f18-8f52-e274e4c55978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6665" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544561} {:answer/id #uuid "e8872e6f-ba5a-570a-bbd3-ab8d4b6496ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544588} {:answer/id #uuid "fba8fc04-0fa8-577c-bd40-5fbcbbc648c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6565" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544456} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c361c4-eefd-5311-9a84-4a9a4e2f34ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bc8f4760-f931-5737-8a9c-5aab0b30c4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-132558" :question/problem "The waiting time for one customer to be served at a cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate of one person served every $4$  minutes. Determine the probability that the next person is served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the waiting time for one customer to be served at the cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until serving the next person as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time needed to serve the next person. The time needed to serve the next person can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since one person is served every $4$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next person will be served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/4}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2/4} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cbb06f0a-ea01-532e-b482-4baad0e7d648" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544916}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45bde8d9-4c6e-5ee0-940a-5de511005e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8647" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544241} {:answer/id #uuid "70f23a81-9ddb-5bb1-b52a-a90210c0f213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8677" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544338} {:answer/id #uuid "75826e41-5f0a-5951-9d1b-962bf48353ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8596" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544348} {:answer/id #uuid "896a0afd-8d66-500b-9100-28316a76192d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8467" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544383} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc1e1f6-fe38-5508-ad8f-639f19a1b5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8417" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544241} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf80934d-3233-573f-8300-6e5e2ddf5899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1455aec3-db48-5efe-b5bf-59b8688bf438" :question/math-academy-id "q-132483" :question/problem "On a webpage, visitors arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $15$ seconds.  Determine the probability of waiting for the next visitor for less than $0.5$ minutes.  Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since visitors arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next visitor appears using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between two consecutive visitor arrivals. Since visitors arrive on average every $15$ seconds, the rate is $\\lambda =60÷15=4$ visitors per minute.\n\nThe time between two consecutive visitor arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(4).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of waiting for the next visitor for less than \n$0.5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}{|}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e2ceb160-b573-55f7-b63a-adf4cc1b5784" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544954}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544072}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180559} :activity/id #uuid "1c5ce268-312c-52a6-9777-1ff390251f96" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180757 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is an affine transformation. The **inverse** of $\\mathbf T$ (if it exists) is an affine transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\\mathbf T^{-1} (\\mathbf T(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf T (\\mathbf T^{-1}(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf u$$\n\nfor every vector $\\mathbf u\\in\\mathbb R^2.$ In other words, the transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ reverses the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\nNot all affine transformations are invertible. So how can we determine whether a given affine transformation is invertible?\n\nTo answer this, recall that an affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ can be expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. We can reverse the action of $\\mathbf T$ by making $\\mathbf u$ the subject of this equation, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ {A}^{-1}\\cdot Au & ={A}^{-1}\\cdot (x-b) \\\\ {I}_{2}\\cdot u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b) \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis equation gives us a method of computing the inverse of an affine transformation. Additionally, we have the following:\n\n*An affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is invertible if and only if the matrix $A$ that represents the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is invertible, that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf99caa7-1e47-5fea-87ac-9b91151af127" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7697 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35dfb0c0-efab-5193-92eb-6eb22cc2af9f" :step/math-academy-id 44660 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179431 :question/id #uuid "f9d3291b-b95b-536b-93ec-9815f7f554d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12428"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378504}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "30275202-8cd8-51e0-a9e1-92aa287dbe42" :step/math-academy-id 44661 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180758 :tutorial/content "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf x} = \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf u} + \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf b}.$$\n\nWe wish to determine whether $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ exists and find its matrix form if it does exist.\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get $$\\det(A) = \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned} \\right| = 1\\cdot (-2) - (-3) \\cdot 1 = 1 \\neq 0.$$\n\nSince $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTo compute the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ we will use the formula we derived earlier:\n\n$$\\mathbf{u} = A^{-1}(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$$\n\nWe start by calculating the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix form of the inverse affine transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4c88a3e-6514-5b56-ab23-306164b0a3a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7698 :tutorial/title "Finding the Inverse of an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "6802d261-21db-5122-886a-1dda8f3a0db4" :step/math-academy-id 44664 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179432 :question/id #uuid "af48b8ba-874a-5753-9874-a2b13fc5a65b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12429"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050944}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378518}] :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "3f8aecda-4d54-5528-939f-886f4ab93593" :step/math-academy-id 44665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179433 :question/id #uuid "c5e70edd-5a39-5e85-9461-68df182780dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12430"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406969}] :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "21d1cffb-5a12-5ee7-ad2a-181e2a8d4474" :step/math-academy-id 44668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179436 :question/id #uuid "98ebb2b6-475b-53c5-acae-0b1b0f0778d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-12441"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049254}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406968}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "35abe35f-9e29-539b-aaa5-a2546fd2fbfb" :step/math-academy-id 44704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180761 :tutorial/content "Consider the square in the $uv$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8cc0923da86a1a1ac6b666893397263ef3865448080500caa6f17510547d901.png)\n\nWe wish to explore the image of this square under an affine transformation $$\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},$$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors.\n\nSimilar to linear transformations, there are three possibilities depending on the value of $\\det(A)$.\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular ($\\det(A)\\neq 0$). In this case, our square is mapped to a parallelogram.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane, we get the following parallelogram:\n\n![](images/36/36da067393fa53f4296373d65e3c6177a54d1a901824188687b4a74ea171afdf.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ has at least one nonzero entry. In this case, our square is mapped to a line segment.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following line segment:\n\n![](images/5e/5e5481e5b2f2218a6097020e260dc991cde05574372c724aa07527458257d94c.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ is the zero matrix. In this case, our square is mapped to a point on the plane.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following point.\n\n![](images/00/0072c5e06674f79f82e771744fda217e6558830f7a6e4ab387aa9dbf4dda09bc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "375d6591-08dd-5dbf-b36a-28013839a28b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7706 :tutorial/title "Image Types Formed by Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "5ec4662b-6870-532f-83f9-f7a9cbe5504e" :step/math-academy-id 44705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180762 :tutorial/content "In general, if we consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the mapping\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, then we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases: \n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane (namely, the point with position vector $\\mathbf b$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "560907bc-354a-5693-a8df-e6824454755d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7710 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Image Types"} :step/id #uuid "d71cf118-7383-5aea-a47c-8359fc7dffe4" :step/math-academy-id 44723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180568}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179860}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406970} :activity/id #uuid "13211b98-9cdf-5456-a254-0f2d03a88cbb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c52d4e-2631-53e7-8a00-72ab68871a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257401} {:answer/id #uuid "746b1cdb-7c35-5efe-a92c-2cd06400438a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257801} {:answer/id #uuid "a34beb49-2bb7-5107-84ce-cae801ce1c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258211} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2b02b6-f203-59a0-ab8f-6e907c4aac2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258857} {:answer/id #uuid "f75572fb-3d5c-57c1-b45a-ea11a4c996bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257801} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e69744-50ea-5409-9359-8197e1464b98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "08a12b2e-6f73-5fc0-987c-f4d58e5bc21f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124241" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ -1 & c\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 8-9\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{5}{4}+(-\\frac{9}{4})+2=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d9d1d09-adca-5f60-87c8-6eda1712954f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc064fc-557b-51b4-acc4-5e5aac84e9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406956} {:answer/id #uuid "394dd73f-cf48-54c7-81fb-4dd53a787045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} {:answer/id #uuid "72653cf4-8cbf-523e-b1d8-e878cfd112ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406961} {:answer/id #uuid "c61e03b3-7008-5b09-9b8a-dd9dc056f91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406964} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b9f565-9eda-5c15-bc7f-e57e8c6bdef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eef93f6-f290-530b-8af5-dd127f8894d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "353e50fd-8521-51a5-ae98-273dc887e6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162221" :question/problem "![](images/99/99b3d68b7f0c89ba430b098f9e7fda95f3013f310060dd71cf86eacc6e51afa7.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the quadrilateral under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} :step/id #uuid "57752ad6-299b-53a6-80c6-38f70d7331f1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acc367e-71f3-5e97-87ce-92842c5b3257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelepiped" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278836} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4b22b4-65fa-5db1-94f1-780f0591f6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279153} {:answer/id #uuid "638bcae2-f573-5b91-97f0-f5ab7fc66eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} {:answer/id #uuid "7e024141-a94e-5f97-ae2f-a04b85d857ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point on the plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279720} {:answer/id #uuid "a42ad425-777b-5f11-8db0-c06039f852ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} :answer-field/id #uuid "2222be89-5b86-5d7e-873f-1d11313d8d91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79161a5e-44bb-5845-8a74-564fa863b598" :question/math-academy-id "q-162151" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f8c1c9854ec54dd3caf4487328d3f3c35f2d69680a8fd1c13d28125dac77d03.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} :step/id #uuid "8adcf20b-9143-567f-8916-5a2f8686af25" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f998697-e815-585e-b0b5-3820b9ab9a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406958} {:answer/id #uuid "51054506-a98c-59d1-82db-eab8461f2cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406959} {:answer/id #uuid "6c76efec-c230-59b8-bab5-b44500517e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406960} {:answer/id #uuid "80f4eee2-e47a-55c2-b5f0-e42eb70ff849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406962} {:answer/id #uuid "c3375fbb-f890-5cba-a4b4-7f53a73a78cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406960} :answer-field/id #uuid "20065c9e-4427-527a-a708-a46b3d2f2065" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e92cb69b-a679-593b-8882-9cab8d26a8fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-124289" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v-1,\\,y=3v-2u-6.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax+y+9,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v-1 \\\\ 3v-2u-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ -2u+3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\\\ y+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot (x+1)+1\\cdot (y+6) \\\\ 2\\cdot (x+1)+1\\cdot (y+6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3x+y+9 \\\\ 2x+y+8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=3x+y+9,\\,v=2x+y+8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+2+8=13.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b7332e33-ee2f-5908-9f85-c258b5d80b5d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406972}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bed1340-8927-5ff0-ba69-b2b93cdd78e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256896} {:answer/id #uuid "81fb632e-14da-5038-b6f9-74ec0da963a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257937} {:answer/id #uuid "82d7f633-617d-5497-a90c-a0550510c2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257941} {:answer/id #uuid "d5507f11-f2dc-54ed-aa00-8c211067e5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} {:answer/id #uuid "e3817db8-3192-5a2c-ad51-eb7cb8e7b55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa29d767-9911-5a05-a828-3b3c81faca3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f20eb077-8cae-5138-ae0e-248f60ff9fb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124280" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2-u-v,\\,y=3u+4v-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=7+ax-y,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y+1) \\\\ 3\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7-4x-y \\\\ 3x+y-5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7+(-4)x-y,\\,v=3x+y+(-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+3+(-5)=-6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c564b67d-ab6c-5a8c-9962-63cefc9e226d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406973}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406955}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412072} :activity/id #uuid "5dadfabb-9118-5878-8b7b-abe67ab51d1a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc064fc-557b-51b4-acc4-5e5aac84e9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406956} {:answer/id #uuid "394dd73f-cf48-54c7-81fb-4dd53a787045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} {:answer/id #uuid "72653cf4-8cbf-523e-b1d8-e878cfd112ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406961} {:answer/id #uuid "c61e03b3-7008-5b09-9b8a-dd9dc056f91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406964} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b9f565-9eda-5c15-bc7f-e57e8c6bdef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eef93f6-f290-530b-8af5-dd127f8894d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "353e50fd-8521-51a5-ae98-273dc887e6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162221" :question/problem "![](images/99/99b3d68b7f0c89ba430b098f9e7fda95f3013f310060dd71cf86eacc6e51afa7.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the quadrilateral under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} :step/id #uuid "57a42c1b-28d8-57a5-acfd-dd199e88f044"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411cf09f-aaa8-5213-a15d-e483b24650c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A tetrahedron" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279170} {:answer/id #uuid "87fc0aa9-2d9d-5344-8ce5-d4d954e92fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279807} {:answer/id #uuid "99cffdbb-a38c-5a63-9d04-87167a638bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279951} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f74625-2c51-5fb6-83b9-61fbae0292bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280479} {:answer/id #uuid "e51121c0-fae9-532e-b1aa-79a388ca1f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279807} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec2e96d-de39-5f66-aab1-77904959b9e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a795336-4ab0-5101-bbca-aa94f4ca2a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-162214" :question/problem "![](images/00/00151dc7a58722a649a52494434950695a27cb4e7cad14f32f76abbddf629a91.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=u-3v+2,\\,y=4v-1.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-3v+2 \\\\ 4v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-3v \\\\ 4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a triangle."} :step/id #uuid "5d41d9ac-1e31-5132-8e1e-f51eb5e05b80" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23580040-99be-5f75-be43-adefd3cbfade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257077} {:answer/id #uuid "58e9a951-d5aa-5d93-8ae3-c1349449a5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257554} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc83444-b44c-54bf-83b7-307d0775c0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258020} {:answer/id #uuid "b1de513c-2a81-54b9-9e95-c28eb012ca2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258331} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c13e37-05bf-5042-b338-1fc4c401c1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257077} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3cd215d-51f0-56ca-9929-c005182aeb03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6d452c80-d748-5af8-bbed-4f41e3a6ab8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124314" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-3v+2,\\,y=6v-3u-4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+a,\\,v=x+by+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-3v+2 \\\\ 6v-3u-4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-3v \\\\ -3u+6v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x-2)+(\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (y+4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+0,\\,v=x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7e79ca9f-a884-5f7c-a928-03d9e0201c9a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bed1340-8927-5ff0-ba69-b2b93cdd78e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256896} {:answer/id #uuid "81fb632e-14da-5038-b6f9-74ec0da963a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257937} {:answer/id #uuid "82d7f633-617d-5497-a90c-a0550510c2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257941} {:answer/id #uuid "d5507f11-f2dc-54ed-aa00-8c211067e5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} {:answer/id #uuid "e3817db8-3192-5a2c-ad51-eb7cb8e7b55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa29d767-9911-5a05-a828-3b3c81faca3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f20eb077-8cae-5138-ae0e-248f60ff9fb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124280" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2-u-v,\\,y=3u+4v-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=7+ax-y,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y+1) \\\\ 3\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7-4x-y \\\\ 3x+y-5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7+(-4)x-y,\\,v=3x+y+(-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+3+(-5)=-6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d9eae084-1f11-516b-9c30-618279f69755" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0086e1d8-f0ee-53c6-8443-35f56170d9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256787} {:answer/id #uuid "0d67fff4-252d-5baa-b919-6e2e71d71094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256912} {:answer/id #uuid "80d9c10d-d8ce-5ef8-9f6d-73c22d791bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257926} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f2b7b2-0f28-5166-96d2-007456d5faee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258602} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a8a822-04f8-5fb6-8bb9-09b853fcf229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258602} :answer-field/id #uuid "79824dff-905f-590c-af91-7c9745e11bfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24203ad0-c666-584c-ace2-35e7e7f927ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-124117" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-2+1+(-8)=-9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e391a44c-ebaa-5dea-a1f8-4caf64dd590c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412071}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412067}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435192} :activity/id #uuid "fe41318c-94a2-58c6-a22a-4e621881d521" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4892ddc7-1f21-5fb6-9203-29d772bde8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257419} {:answer/id #uuid "92a426f4-8720-57e6-a1e8-ad581c1828da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258075} {:answer/id #uuid "9f843863-e410-57ac-ad2a-c4d41c982c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258183} {:answer/id #uuid "a1613b31-d49f-5ba6-a356-2221335e1c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258196} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fab22d-ecf7-5ea8-a60c-5286e5e43015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258411} :answer-field/id #uuid "da74548d-3633-5796-b2bf-ea07c5e41132" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8b41b10b-145c-5e96-a5f6-3186f40b3311" :question/math-academy-id "q-124244" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 5 \\\\ b & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-3+(-1)+8=4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b672460-44db-5bc9-bbbb-d13ca12e5373" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435193}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "709e3e28-43f0-5774-9967-f2a77296b518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257772} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5307ee-7945-53c6-8b5c-e3c7b40d7f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257864} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a72ca3-839f-596e-9ba3-db4dee4a50f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258642} {:answer/id #uuid "deb6b58c-00ac-51db-9e70-4039e8ace72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258721} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ff2522-63f2-5ecd-8947-0cdfd4cb6b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258721} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed49f22-b423-5b9a-b990-ba7eb27d6825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c388fe2c-958c-51fb-bcca-cd64f6bc965e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124224" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ a & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1\\cdot (-9)-2\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+(-9)+20=7.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "55933838-8190-5662-8692-814c8a886119" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435191}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23580040-99be-5f75-be43-adefd3cbfade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257077} {:answer/id #uuid "58e9a951-d5aa-5d93-8ae3-c1349449a5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257554} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc83444-b44c-54bf-83b7-307d0775c0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258020} {:answer/id #uuid "b1de513c-2a81-54b9-9e95-c28eb012ca2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258331} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c13e37-05bf-5042-b338-1fc4c401c1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257077} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3cd215d-51f0-56ca-9929-c005182aeb03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6d452c80-d748-5af8-bbed-4f41e3a6ab8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124314" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-3v+2,\\,y=6v-3u-4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+a,\\,v=x+by+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-3v+2 \\\\ 6v-3u-4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-3v \\\\ -3u+6v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x-2)+(\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (y+4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+0,\\,v=x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7c2cc732-641c-57e5-95b3-542bab8f0800"}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435190}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445237} :activity/id #uuid "8d550678-9055-5adb-a6ff-5b7c64c11448" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2f4802-202c-5744-af7c-a265e1282f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257190} {:answer/id #uuid "6145b310-d9fa-5c70-bf9e-37a7eee79a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257636} {:answer/id #uuid "6565c78d-4700-5b5e-8fa6-fa3c4fceeb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257675} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7de5ee-ba45-55a9-a92f-e771483fbdc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257917} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c4affd-1411-548e-b075-bcc64b41f0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257190} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ef3bd3-deb2-573a-a0a0-ddce62489c56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2c660cc6-fa1c-504a-b604-d474fb35da3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124315" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=5u-9v-2,\\,y=4u-8v+4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-\\frac{9}{4}y+b,\\,v=cx-\\frac{5}{4}y+7.\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u-9v-2 \\\\ 4u-8v+4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u-9v \\\\ 4u-8v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-8\\cdot 5-(-9)\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-4}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x+2 \\\\ y-4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (x+2)-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (y-4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+2)+(-\\frac{5}{4})\\cdot (y-4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x-\\frac{9}{4}y+13 \\\\ x-\\frac{5}{4}y+7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-\\frac{9}{4}y+13,\\,v=1x-\\frac{5}{4}y+7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+13+1=16.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1e5edc5f-97f4-5214-8e0a-72cb196631dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acc367e-71f3-5e97-87ce-92842c5b3257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelepiped" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278836} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4b22b4-65fa-5db1-94f1-780f0591f6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279153} {:answer/id #uuid "638bcae2-f573-5b91-97f0-f5ab7fc66eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} {:answer/id #uuid "7e024141-a94e-5f97-ae2f-a04b85d857ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point on the plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279720} {:answer/id #uuid "a42ad425-777b-5f11-8db0-c06039f852ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} :answer-field/id #uuid "2222be89-5b86-5d7e-873f-1d11313d8d91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79161a5e-44bb-5845-8a74-564fa863b598" :question/math-academy-id "q-162151" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f8c1c9854ec54dd3caf4487328d3f3c35f2d69680a8fd1c13d28125dac77d03.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} :step/id #uuid "31efd4db-96ca-5899-a79c-9be17347f5b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445239}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a8b2e4c-71f4-525e-882c-30ff8c65e625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257299} {:answer/id #uuid "6713e9ff-c15a-5839-bf29-fa79fff395dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257695} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6d52a8-e329-53f2-9470-69f201f764c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257744} {:answer/id #uuid "db338955-c82e-5393-9733-8fbd662c8303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258696} {:answer/id #uuid "dc799e2e-0aae-57e8-a311-8b305ba143a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258703}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257744} :answer-field/id #uuid "837832a8-e75c-560a-ae2c-b1843afa6adc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "51a6b16d-03c2-54f1-bf60-c27ba81220b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124228" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & -2 \\\\ -4 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-4)}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-7+(-1)+3=-5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "320920d1-2f71-59ff-899e-943cde1b5fe3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04abb601-17e0-5fec-b990-97706ac5a596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256822} {:answer/id #uuid "20b7769d-6be5-53ae-a10f-0d9f4c90d0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257049} {:answer/id #uuid "525f19b0-d029-5095-845b-fc54069a98b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257485} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffe1822-1c33-543b-8781-69059dae9e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257765} {:answer/id #uuid "97f74daa-bcde-5c29-bc67-733be230ca01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258121}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257049} :answer-field/id #uuid "a412914a-2d2e-597f-8fb0-48dccc9cd9de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ab7706f1-5139-5e95-9482-c097bef51185" :question/math-academy-id "q-124282" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-v+1,\\,y=9u-4v+3.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=y-4x+a,\\,v=by+cx+3,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v+1 \\\\ 9u-4v+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 9u-4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 9 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 9}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\cdot (x-1)+1\\cdot (y-3) \\\\ -9\\cdot (x-1)+2\\cdot (y-3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}y-4x+1 \\\\ 2y-9x+3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=y-4x+1,\\,v=2y+(-9)x+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+2+(-9)=-6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33e812b7-0a19-5479-b824-948d87ff1605" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445235}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e04b870d-e25b-5d96-b606-28a110148002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389259} {:answer/id #uuid "062427a9-0f48-53fc-94f1-c6c3d5e866d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389260} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b5ad5a-830d-59fb-be20-4b48087915f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389261} {:answer/id #uuid "4003098a-783e-5e93-bb25-5f94f36652a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389262} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7e5e40-3f3f-5c2d-abac-4a9137d31956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389262} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1250926-eea3-55ee-b530-0094a4347a99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b31d6cdc-6f82-507f-838b-9eddf7b3f11a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162215" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f550963f047a3860566564abc2cb1e9f3939125895912762ff751767ca55c57.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=2u-v,\\,y=4u-v+2.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible, and the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a pentagon."} :step/id #uuid "86b72ad4-07e7-5b0a-8c51-311053773e89"}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445234}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125429} :activity/id #uuid "f4d9b1d3-776c-5de2-b62c-cbb016b96f1e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048726 :topic/id #uuid "b8f25367-71a5-50d2-9e6e-d239937a1fac" :topic/math-academy-id 1972} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125794 :tutorial/content "The characteristic equation of a square matrix $A$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the left-hand side of this equation, we have the expression \n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I),\n$$\n\n which we call the **characteristic polynomial** of the matrix $A.$\n\nFor instance, let's find the characteristic polynomial of the matrix ![](images/1d/1d6d0bdedbb25bbc41759e443cdc0f6a207ef5cbfa4761b05e1a1a2eda24cbec.svg)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(C-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =(-8-\\lambda )\\cdot (7-\\lambda )-(-9)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "25396b96-19ec-592d-b1c4-3c6fa172ef08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3580 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9083e01a-4e57-589b-9b08-48ecc4af0075" :step/math-academy-id 20133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125795 :tutorial/content "The **Cayley-Hamilton theorem** states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $\\,n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix whose characteristic polynomial is* \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I) = (-1)^n\\lambda^n + c_{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1}+\\cdots+c_1\\lambda + c_0.\n$$\n\n*Then, the matrix $A$ is a solution of the characteristic equation $p(\\lambda) = 0$.* \n\nThat is to say, if we substitute $A$ into the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)$, taking $A^0 = I,$ then we have\n\n$$\np(A) = (-1)^nA^n + c_{n-1}A^{n-1}+\\cdots+c_1A + c_0I = O,\n$$\n\nwhere $O$ is the zero-matrix. \n\nFor example, according to this theorem, for the matrix \n![](images/d2/d24f480293267a9ddc28b03626e7bde6f01bd62a136fed17f8580bf1ea34d776.svg)\nwhose characteristic polynomial is \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2 +\\lambda -2,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\np(A) = A^2 +A -2I =O.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86101838-75af-5a3f-9dae-1e63a94c77a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3581 :tutorial/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7b3b4f95-8159-5533-bd80-db194716f75b" :step/math-academy-id 20134 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125432}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124345 :question/id #uuid "c262f28d-bfd0-5c78-bce7-d2ae314052e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5510"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430412}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"} :step/id #uuid "d9e0bce9-63bf-58e4-af9b-2bb5b5d8e03c" :step/math-academy-id 20135 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125432 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124537 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124346 :question/id #uuid "8e6d3017-d05d-5a2d-8043-c827e612476e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5511"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430413}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"} :step/id #uuid "1c6e6482-d051-50c1-b91d-40d9a95a380d" :step/math-academy-id 20136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125796 :tutorial/content "What conclusions can we make regarding the characteristic polynomial of the general $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/8e/8e26937ef1981804a548e92a4041bd8672cc75c7c5cc6efa0c0c2694c0736791.svg)\n\nFirst, let's find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\left|\\begin{aligned}a-\\lambda  & b \\\\ c & d-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| & =0 \\\\ (a-\\lambda )(d-\\lambda )-bc & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-(a+d)\\lambda +(ad-bc) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term is the determinant of the matrix: \n\n$$\n\\det(A) = ad-bc\n$$\n\n \n\nThe coefficient of $\\lambda$ is the sum of the diagonal entries of $A$ taken with a negative sign. The sum of the diagonal entries of a matrix $A$ is called the **trace** of $A,$ and we denote it as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{tr}(A) = a+d.\n$$\n\nSo, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^2 -\\mathrm{tr}(A)\\lambda + \\det(A).\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result can be extended to the general case of $n \\times n$ matrices. If $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, then its characteristic polynomial takes the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{c}_{n-1}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{c}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{{(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{n-1}}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{{det(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{0}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{n-1}=(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{0}=det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1c54b7a6-10ea-5c57-ad4e-245a308d4790" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3582 :tutorial/title "The Characteristic Polynomial for 2x2 Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "4e500723-739c-51c7-b82e-8727a24a1b1f" :step/math-academy-id 20137 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125455}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125434 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124538 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124347 :question/id #uuid "ce724b3b-f440-5c1f-86a6-c50a54931233" :question/math-academy-id "e-5512"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050117}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392225}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "0fed3d43-139d-505f-8482-70f3be0516d6" :step/math-academy-id 20138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124552 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124361 :question/id #uuid "95b44b17-b796-51eb-93dd-4ee4b2e5c3f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5894"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050669}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486439}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f3faf291-dd20-56c9-bc3d-8482113b6764" :step/math-academy-id 21546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125430}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125824 :tutorial/content "If we want to evaluate a high-degree polynomial for a given matrix input, it can take a lot of time. For example, suppose we wanted to evaluate the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^{10} - 5x^9 - 2x^8 + x + 2\n$$\n\nfor the input ![](images/0f/0f9bff826261a956305962b4ca945ed91012cc5042e6ffe92a4b5f1ce13d3237.svg) Computing the $10$th power of this matrix would be a lot of work!\n\nLuckily, there is a way to speed up the process. The trick is to use Cayley-Hamilton theorem, which tells us that every matrix is a solution to its characteristic equation.\n\nFor the given matrix ![](images/65/6514096b22aed47005e5ff59c47e3cec701aed5b120b0022b186f1bec941d953.svg) the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-6 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the Cayley-Hamilton theorem tells us that\n\n$$\np(A) = A^2 - 5A - 2I = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, observe that the polynomial $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{10}-5{x}^{9}-2{x}^{8}+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{8}({x}^{2}-5x-2)+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{8}p(x)+x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = A^8 p(A) + A + 2I,\n$$\n\nand because $p(A) = 0,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{8}\\cdot 0+A+2I \\\\  & =0+A+2I \\\\  & =A+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the computation is much faster:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =A+2I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Other times, it may not be so obvious how to group the terms of $f(x)$ to reveal $p(x).$ In the general case, it's best to divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division, and use the result\n\n$$\nf(x) = q(x) p(x) + r(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the remainder. Let's see an example of this." :tutorial/id #uuid "769ceb10-1667-52b6-8a89-b4c7ca835276" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4448 :tutorial/title "Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem to Quickly Evaluate a Matrix Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "3167e19f-42e7-5fc7-8ee9-fb73a5fa3da3" :step/math-academy-id 27370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125434}}] :activity/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124757}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440844} :activity/id #uuid "08986419-9a71-537f-9305-f8e778b26413" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048726 :topic/id #uuid "b8f25367-71a5-50d2-9e6e-d239937a1fac" :topic/math-academy-id 1972} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b7c0e75-3d49-5406-9041-ede1d5bfdf47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351962} {:answer/id #uuid "33bb1eb5-07b0-5c72-9e4a-a39b341c088c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352048} {:answer/id #uuid "6b960fbb-7fd4-564b-88d9-596a86197fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352519} {:answer/id #uuid "a471234f-1f9f-56ed-9886-d314b4205faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352971} {:answer/id #uuid "e3378a1b-e1c9-5e1d-b90b-a6157d273dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351962} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba728d29-f974-5f4e-bf33-9c1c26f466d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "83ac805a-2248-5c38-94ec-8370eb490b39" :question/math-academy-id "q-85928" :question/problem "Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-7A+8I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-7A+8I) & =O \\\\ A-7I+8{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{8}I-\\frac{1}{8}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{7}{8},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{8}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{7}{8}+(-\\frac{1}{8})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "37974a8d-1c28-54c7-b296-b5b3c2e14d09" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23996cc9-567a-51b9-98b0-2f94757720ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{2}-3A+16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351889} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9440a6-7475-5119-a281-5c4281a46f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{2}-3A+6I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352117} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e14de5-77a9-57b7-8420-3ce37cbf93ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3A-16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352952} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a9477d-b279-5e07-bb55-bcddccdb5c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{2}+3A-16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353124} {:answer/id #uuid "dedacf6f-daed-557d-84e3-a6bbb9be9999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3A+16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351889} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb1e7ca4-8013-510f-a476-bdfe0c462d57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "03d6fc6a-0f72-5d35-ba67-c4e4d25be4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-85924" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -16.$ Then, ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{3}-{A}^{2}+3A-16I & =O \\\\ {A}^{3} & ={A}^{2}-3A+16I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "864708dc-01cf-5025-81a5-3d649155dedc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28cc5ee2-6da9-52c1-a92b-8edb26634139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324300} {:answer/id #uuid "47e93b3f-33d7-5090-b1fc-02844e927bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324553} {:answer/id #uuid "a44579f4-a9e4-53da-a506-9eefbb4bf2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325367} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8f6f17-d42e-553d-a74b-0e76268b5611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325601} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b95922-aad1-5b77-a0d0-e6a698ab713b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324300} :answer-field/id #uuid "36035083-9336-58a8-9968-10256743cb12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "688a2834-9fcd-5275-97a0-97e7aaa1b79c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38704" :question/problem "Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+6I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+6I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+6{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 6{A}^{-1} & =8I-A \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{4}{3}I-\\frac{1}{6}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{4}{3},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{6}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{4}{3}+(-\\frac{1}{6})=\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8b979659-38ec-5258-8754-0f5aa34455d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c632238-e62f-5f74-b0da-ba8a2b7c7882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 0 \\\\ 72 & -27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351821} {:answer/id #uuid "470c7209-55c8-5c45-abce-7fe3f84f4c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 27 \\\\ 0 & -72\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352224} {:answer/id #uuid "5e13d16b-8e89-589c-b83e-8c447ea31fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}27 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352401} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3d6b3f-3399-57a6-94b9-b1ab92b58f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353035} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1b1292-04d5-5403-8881-a67fccaa7676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353490}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353035} :answer-field/id #uuid "af398d18-f5a4-5a12-bc2f-96080c170593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e1de5236-83e9-5753-abb0-71abaf264e50" :question/math-academy-id "q-85993" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $f(x)={x}^{21}-16{x}^{19}+6x-3$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda ) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,{x}^{19}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}-16\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{21}\\,-\\,16{x}^{19}\\,+\\,6x\\,\\,-\\,\\,3\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{21}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$16{x}^{19}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$6x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(6x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{19}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =6A-3I \\\\  & =6\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -24\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9ee92d90-8ec9-5c83-abd0-9bf2b158360d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025a5c1a-4f6e-5e42-8dce-96a2fd08178a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -42 \\\\ 14 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269289} {:answer/id #uuid "23310913-8cd2-557c-be76-7e2691fe0ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}31 & 14 \\\\ 42 & -25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269575} {:answer/id #uuid "9142a6e7-f5cb-5d0d-bcaf-2c7c6f384044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 14 \\\\ -42 & 30\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270533} {:answer/id #uuid "efb97e7a-5224-5bc1-9035-2d0d54445cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 12 \\\\ -24 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271376} {:answer/id #uuid "f7416ab1-633c-5a86-bade-cffdf59d2d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271438}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271438} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d72f403-dcf6-5b06-9505-8ae246f18a3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "af2db92b-ede0-52d7-a7d4-1043a63d9db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-140602" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{16}-4{x}^{14}+7x-3\\,$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+12 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,{x}^{14}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}-4\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{16}\\,-\\,4{x}^{14}\\,+\\,7x\\,-\\,3\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{16}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$4{x}^{14}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$7x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{14}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =7A-3I \\\\  & =7\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -28\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a1707eef-06e0-5503-8342-6b9421462ee5"}] :activity/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440841}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486487} :activity/id #uuid "335bb2d0-e529-5cbe-ad74-695b640df1d6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048726 :topic/id #uuid "b8f25367-71a5-50d2-9e6e-d239937a1fac" :topic/math-academy-id 1972} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bf09fd-3fd5-5fa9-bd7c-9bb8300133ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{3}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324178} {:answer/id #uuid "2196ad3b-dc18-575d-9ac5-f3e82f2cf1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324248} {:answer/id #uuid "ae47339b-004d-5c20-8d9d-8aab1f4f98f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2I-A-2{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325449} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9f773a-008b-571e-8538-a3402ba6e607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}I-\\frac{7}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325590} {:answer/id #uuid "d899671a-8147-5a25-9c47-966430b53806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9I-A-{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324248} :answer-field/id #uuid "f00c9e90-925d-5d82-b4f9-f2d77c2b5637" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d383f46-698f-5924-8d8d-4466dfa087b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40168" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )+3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+A-7I+2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 2{A}^{-1} & =7I-A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "29abcc68-95f8-5957-a518-0a35c26f28c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486479 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "486030d8-debf-5bf3-8646-937755b93331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324558} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb39a68-d9fa-5afc-a46e-1646bc050802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{A}^{2}-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325023} {:answer/id #uuid "8f700b32-f8f5-50c2-8448-a2e3950f6cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{A}^{2}-I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325188} {:answer/id #uuid "d2abfec0-1ad1-5263-bbad-6dc6f697cbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2A-I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325799} {:answer/id #uuid "ec99ac0a-a7c2-59e7-88f3-37b0874d74a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2A-I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326052}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326052} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae27e9b-e1c9-591e-ae97-64fbf12bf64f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "241347e1-d70b-502b-97bb-5ffa2634f0a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38723" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=-{\\lambda }^{3}-2\\lambda -1.$ Then, ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ -{A}^{3}-2A-I & =O \\\\ {A}^{3} & =-2A-I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4d30d155-c0af-5612-bddd-5c0adca752a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d94a386-0e3f-5fd6-82d2-7e3b9d7cbeec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321252} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbc3e40-f109-58b8-872a-95626283c9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321493} {:answer/id #uuid "389d0b0e-b88a-5ab8-9fa3-85ae37139eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321578} {:answer/id #uuid "73fc0bbe-4d33-5b94-bad1-7a748598633e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322053} {:answer/id #uuid "f9936d7e-e12d-5d02-a132-90f3f19d81e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323111}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321252} :answer-field/id #uuid "399304a9-7f1c-5273-8e27-ae2b68d805f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a72555f5-f964-563d-a2c6-2e5bad5e5508" :question/math-academy-id "q-37778" :question/problem "Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-4A+I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-4A+I) & =O \\\\ A-4I+{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =4I-A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4,$ $q=-1$ and\n\n$$\np+q=4+(-1)=3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "712962aa-1f8a-5106-8afe-8f1d72596893" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486479}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee48dc36-244e-5623-8162-9e5def5de475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -22 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392227} {:answer/id #uuid "4e10d15f-edfd-50e4-802f-39336ec76684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392228} {:answer/id #uuid "7b98b20b-4948-5630-912d-d10854621cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392229} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ce0307-f7b7-5df0-93a8-a3b0f9edcea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -22 \\\\ 18 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392230} {:answer/id #uuid "52829169-8b9a-5477-a7ca-27875b4c0680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-22 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392229} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2234961-7a5f-5c89-bdd3-f09c2cf9ab03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4c6e1cb3-d3e5-5ac7-9b61-9f6c0c45ae57" :question/math-academy-id "q-38728" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $f(x)={x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(6-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )+33 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,{x}^{13}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}-3\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{15}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{13}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,5x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,3x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,2x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(-2x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{13}+x.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(-2A+6I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(-2A+6I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-2A+6I) \\\\  & =-2A+6I \\\\  & =-2\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "aa57e7bc-ae6a-50ca-b096-5a8dfd3e3450" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069c8840-8426-5a49-8cd5-194f9542f047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485779} {:answer/id #uuid "2e481c8d-1f08-54f2-a266-5bbd2762473f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485851} {:answer/id #uuid "93d03cdd-069e-5ebe-b2a7-900eec2dd6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486030} {:answer/id #uuid "a74c6630-89ff-50d5-a8b8-e49610f1abd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486067} {:answer/id #uuid "b94c247d-0ad1-594f-abae-d08d3203dd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486099} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58af114-b2ae-5846-9a75-5301fb89508f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c746a2db-cfe0-519b-a0f3-936f85b88bb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140520" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix},$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 7-(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =35+12 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f73f0739-b0b6-5a0b-9fac-ab5fdff3e3e1"}] :activity/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485755}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130071} :activity/id #uuid "40540042-d3fd-5add-a52b-682e9eaa6e90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048986 :topic/id #uuid "d11c4451-1bcb-5f1a-a6d3-3f5fdbe85bb7" :topic/math-academy-id 1997} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130399 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the repeated integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\color{black}0}^{\\color{black}2} \\int_{\\color{black}x/2}^{\\color{black}1} \\, e^{y^2}\\,\\textrm{d}{\\color{black}y} \\, \\textrm{d}{\\color{black}x}.\n$$\n\nThe expression \"$\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x\\,$\" tells us that we should integrate first with respect to $y.$ This is problematic, though, as the integrand $e^{y^2}$ does not have an elementary antiderivative. So, what can we do?\n\nIn cases like this, **reversing the order of integration** from $\\, \\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x\\,$ to $\\, \\textrm{d}x\\,\\textrm{d}y$ might help. But beware! Because the domain of integration is non-rectangular, we can't just swap the order of the integrals. This is because, to integrate with respect to $x,$ we can't have $x$'s in the limits. So we need to work out new limits.\n\nWhen swapping the order of integration, it is helpful to sketch the region $D$ over which we're integrating.\n\n![](images/d3/d3ee76083ae3917b4585fce7227475d3e0471550f193f2fa437be3ebf92dc288.png)\n\nTo integrate first with respect to $y,$ we imagine a line parallel to the $y$-axis, as shown above. Note the following:\n\n- This line *enters* the region through the curve $\\color{black} y=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ and *leaves* at $\\color{black} y=1.$ These are the variable limits for $y.$\n\n- The minimum value of $x$ is $\\color{black}0,$ and the maximum value of $x$ is ${\\color{black}2}.$\n\nSo, the integral we were initially given is over the following type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\left \\{  (x,y) \\: : \\: {\\color{black}0} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{black}2}, \\quad {\\color{black}\\dfrac{x}{2}} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{black}1} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we now want to integrate first with respect to $x,$ we need the limits for $x$ to be functions of $y$, and we need the limits of $y$ to be constants. To work these out, we redraw the region, but this time we imagine a line parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/c8/c80ae995a92aba4f535b5e5a01305f8ffbf790ad1f97ee8db4f368e4bfabb0c1.png)\n\n- The line *enters* the region at $\\color{black}x=0$ and *leaves* the region at $\\color{black} x=2y.$ So these are our variable limits for $x.$\n\n- The minimum value of $\\color{black}y$ is $\\color{black}0,$ and the maximum value of $y$ is ${\\color{black}1}.$\n\nSo, we can consider $D$ to be the following type II region:\n\n$$\nD=\\left \\{  (x,y):\\quad {\\color{black}0} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{black}2y}, \\quad {\\color{black}0} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{black}1} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressed the region $D$ as a type II region, we're ready to swap the order of integration and finally calculate our integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{1}\\,{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{y}^{2}}[x]_{0}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y{e}^{{y}^{2}}dy \\\\  & ={[{e}^{{y}^{2}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ccfd99d2-4e17-53c3-8ac5-2394c59be5c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4357 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e13e6db5-65c2-53ea-9644-faae6ec26f16" :step/math-academy-id 26536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130074}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129189 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129008 :question/id #uuid "9b2415da-21b8-5a59-9b3f-2f30b624c36f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7194"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1723a1-c676-517c-8dc5-f2b61ba017c6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488189}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "d45a7233-e073-55c6-8a71-375178f2f11a" :step/math-academy-id 26537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129190 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129009 :question/id #uuid "3477b5ca-309c-5b2a-b3fd-41dfc9bbf4db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7195"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f52a0604-77f9-51c5-948a-710c52c26fe7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049003}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544876}] :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region"} :step/id #uuid "981fea3c-a71a-5567-bcaf-63176b8545f5" :step/math-academy-id 26538 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130072}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129010 :question/id #uuid "eab89095-f5e5-53dc-9141-0f1a876f8a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7196"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475945}] :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Non-Partitioned Region"} :step/id #uuid "768398f5-6108-549e-9288-fd51dc694028" :step/math-academy-id 26540 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130073}}] :activity/title "Changing the Order of Integration in Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129342}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488297} :activity/id #uuid "bf5c1cdc-1231-5945-ae88-c7df3fc76214" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048986 :topic/id #uuid "d11c4451-1bcb-5f1a-a6d3-3f5fdbe85bb7" :topic/math-academy-id 1997} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab8fcd9-4602-5070-8690-3160b7afea7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487621} {:answer/id #uuid "295507dd-328a-5bb3-964f-3c0f304a9d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487650} {:answer/id #uuid "335927a5-38d1-5363-b0b7-58dbc7940dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(1-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487664} {:answer/id #uuid "6465b0aa-6d02-553e-acdc-7035e0eb70ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487768} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f6c3ee-d01c-5d14-87c0-fce9876c9b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487650} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eba9098-a15f-5710-a728-b6dfa4ffcdb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "18010b03-c75c-5a16-8ddc-495cf577e016" :question/math-academy-id "q-38799" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/54/54727a51e88b3543726b61606a0f41b9a8cf5ec46ef4c69d7233fd54a4b50242.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/95/956aaf81973d9a2b3e56082ecae8e654eee55d6f99c42dd433d126632d7c7262.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{y}^{4}}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{{y}^{4}}}{3}[{x}^{3}]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[{e}^{{y}^{4}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1e312c9f-cb74-5f7f-9c13-ab33fcd2a8b6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13af14b5-15a1-566b-a656-d8a378d7320c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324147} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1a2f03-596f-5e6f-978b-605dcec2e3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324215} {:answer/id #uuid "860bc5a9-e2eb-5f7c-a636-43aba8e0881d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{4-y}^{y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325106} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fec665-57db-5f83-bb6e-f9267a2d10aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{4-y}^{y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325336} {:answer/id #uuid "f2804b9b-bae3-55b0-8ce1-7ac7c4d428a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326107} :answer-field/id #uuid "759c1086-e737-5e21-a10a-cebde7f9cab4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "51957ec1-4df6-5139-b864-fabbb3776e09" :question/math-academy-id "q-38601" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/ea36f1ec1ddeb03dd8d133fb8d65b5d50b3fa07ba34495978d87f95742dee042.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-x\\,⟹\\,x=4-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e82098a4bb63f1d709a06b6a24da078594716d6a3cc0fa2e069af9dcd9154955.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "32fd6dca-607a-5214-a4a3-fbde7a7bf2c4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1215436d-cf83-5f92-b39e-a9bec379f2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y/2}}{\\int }_{(4-y)/2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487602} {:answer/id #uuid "1291e1c1-cc7f-5dab-8331-0c5c3605b71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{(4-y)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487605} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce71b5a-ae72-56f3-907b-b226cfb92e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{(4-y)/2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487782} {:answer/id #uuid "a34f841b-c478-5350-b05a-4956614fb33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{(4-y)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487872} {:answer/id #uuid "a838ae42-337e-55e8-9ec4-76976d1219db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{(4-y)/2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc9b43e-629a-5bba-b51a-3bf554da7329" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "291bba76-b18a-519c-8c81-930cc19f299e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82253" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/83/8359a189981b238d0851324a896ed0a3a9aa0620e8cf4d38228752ff484b4cd0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2{x}^{2}\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2(x-2)^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=2{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{4-y}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\\le x\\le \\frac{4-y}{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/be/bee5de8ca9011672a159686d1547a909e5288097b15241f5004e5c788459ee70.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2(x-2)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{(4-y)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48d26477-5711-5c8c-9f6f-58692fb49ba5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12862e2a-8176-5480-9c3a-b73349d4eada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487603} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfe33b9-51d1-5788-8662-58bc8ab68510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487684} {:answer/id #uuid "469a96e1-ead2-518e-bb41-99b1ce5ab6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487704} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfda9e5-2b1f-5351-b880-190a9511ab54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487811} {:answer/id #uuid "ff35cb83-3ce5-547e-a2d3-554a83f4034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487704} :answer-field/id #uuid "acad3c64-a048-5588-ba2a-180649362504" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1781d10d-4447-567d-8dfd-9f16c098a159" :question/math-academy-id "q-132647" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d2/d2ef7f656cf706636c82e1ef41ae338a596fff5142962a74875f762301937932.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le y\\le -1,\\,1\\le x\\le -\\frac{4}{y}\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=-1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{4}{y}\\,⟹\\,y=-\\frac{4}{x},\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{4}{x}\\le y\\le -1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/33/3338ee0f8bdc6f18bb5a56b12a36d31d79c87db6c39686adc7ae7c00efb9d526.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-4/x}^{-1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}[{\\int }_{-4/x}^{-1}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{-4/x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{4}{x}-1)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-4+2}-2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-1+2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2}-2 \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "66d3bee5-2c83-50d6-baeb-1f27e2bd6de0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "092a29e0-d74d-5d43-953c-1f5d9bf2a5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388295} {:answer/id #uuid "60cc9f6b-b572-50b9-952f-12e386a01c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388296} {:answer/id #uuid "aea1d3dc-a5bf-519d-8f6d-ded54403dbfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388297} {:answer/id #uuid "133a0e32-0ea5-5f11-8c08-78665b249cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388298} {:answer/id #uuid "81d539a4-fe56-54af-a346-d28ee3e104cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388295} :answer-field/id #uuid "66942a40-bd6e-5606-91e2-d8c0f63814d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9a7843d-3020-5482-8aef-e872811d9165" :question/math-academy-id "q-132565" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1-e}^{0}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/34/3426e7bdd134bc9af5a3665f8c9c55a65514afa9dca5be112eabb37479c4c9ec.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type II regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1-e\\le y\\le 0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)\\le x\\le 1\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le e-1,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\le x\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,⟹\\,y={e}^{x}-1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)\\,⟹\\,y=1-{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1-{e}^{x}\\le y\\le {e}^{x}-1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7d/7d88d351b481859ec3e77350d3679387836d2e7f67325caca50e2f61cce9dcce.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1-e}^{0}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a31ece2d-f9c7-54ca-957f-b6feaf1a1054" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488310}}] :activity/title "Changing the Order of Integration in Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487562}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544913} :activity/id #uuid "4fe3e7fe-66b2-56e4-b07b-e2a8d0dd2aac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048986 :topic/id #uuid "d11c4451-1bcb-5f1a-a6d3-3f5fdbe85bb7" :topic/math-academy-id 1997} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83af4c85-31c8-5333-8b0c-d044a02bd3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{x+1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267303} {:answer/id #uuid "8835a210-9de5-5c5f-a9a0-4264ee64fa8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{x+1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267345} {:answer/id #uuid "bda7f1f6-caae-5b5a-912d-ad3b965431f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{x+1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267847} {:answer/id #uuid "c79e2d71-f72b-5349-9f02-4326a7a438da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{-x-1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267934} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9ca14f-93c5-5b93-88bb-be41c8973bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{-x-1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267934} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfe90643-e048-5d75-985f-e9ed17b022bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "88491dc6-a6a9-5e2a-ab57-c63ec8866fd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132574" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}-1}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/92/926e4c717707d13fdb9562e149d2f41c3ab657b42fbae35c0953019ab93a06b6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,-{y}^{2}-1\\le x\\le -y\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](images/89/8933233e8aca4a36ee90ffec69ca6e93d0523d3fa50c400d2cdea78f9ec80812.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $y=y(x),$  are \n\n$\\,$ $y=\\sqrt{-x-1}\\,$ and $\\,y=1.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=-x.$ \n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le -1,\\,\\sqrt{-x-1}\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le -x\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}-1}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{-x-1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "19c28e52-fdaf-5ad1-b61f-5be844edc12c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1158bf19-5d87-5d3c-ae18-4b6a90a7b40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1+{e}^{-4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544121} {:answer/id #uuid "3290e5fa-709a-5cf8-9205-a80c7e9bb0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1-{e}^{-4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544199} {:answer/id #uuid "8d52f326-a07f-5846-9198-2281c9cb0ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544391} {:answer/id #uuid "fc472a25-8431-51bb-a98c-291448f6d760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544638} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3e3379-5ac5-5f47-9142-c0dd3eb25756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1+e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544199} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ae90e2c-9b42-50f3-a4dd-8cea89d355b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1b66dc5-759e-5b16-9d35-fc67c34a4499" :question/math-academy-id "q-38466" :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/79/79326f0f463d3b26db4ab497045fa35aa5aa7fb68be6fd79b5ebd633a4d6646f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y\\le x\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=y\\,⟹\\,y=x,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. Its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/44/4452d48cac6bd387e8f7f27a9aedfbe51f9c5e478a5288f889aa3378c4805d34.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}[y]_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-{e}^{-4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2ea8c9b7-08dd-5aab-ac0c-8a662422b3d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32fbf319-3234-58a8-95fb-c53b859ed56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348902} {:answer/id #uuid "49820777-b895-52a8-9251-7d27144fa1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-{e}^{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349114} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6fed8a-5620-59e2-bea5-d6cea14b66c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349455} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe1cda9-fe65-5b36-99c7-49ccecf2e000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{16}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349473} {:answer/id #uuid "f9126db6-0a47-59b1-b887-454248d2f098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350634}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349473} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55be9a3-3b8c-5e73-9f00-cfe58e1d5da1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0524dddc-0711-51cc-bea5-211d12fb3f76" :question/math-academy-id "q-82296" :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{8}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d5/d5d777577f85cd577ce35bfd3a99c1e2218622d1856f4fe7ee4afe1cfd2c9f86.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 8,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le x\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. Its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05ec37a8f4fd33c05a2438df5c5fbc697f8b560fde0b9fe73922a0db56a09c2c.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}[y]_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{{x}^{4}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{16}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2ef3a076-a0cb-5e87-9202-566dbdfa7430" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad97339-5570-5d24-b1fb-77ec680fac1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2-y}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544142} {:answer/id #uuid "55f54e57-e18b-5d0c-9c19-13b3bfe26010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544276} {:answer/id #uuid "6a97f4e4-20e0-58ad-87bc-d933151f4bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544319} {:answer/id #uuid "bcca3de2-d84e-525d-b4d1-8d4e8c0a5627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{y}}{\\int }_{2-y}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544487} {:answer/id #uuid "c31f4f8b-4dd6-5e15-bb60-2549b490b990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544319} :answer-field/id #uuid "d330fe8c-c0b4-581d-86c1-4285baf2910e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "437b2f48-85ba-532a-9ac5-52a828cb51ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-82254" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/47/472aa6b0afe20f7c1902446102265f758637c9ec00eb985bf38a658dc92f3fe5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=2-x\\,⟹\\,x=2-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le x\\le 2-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/77/77b59ce5c40514338b0d23a31e7028c7fa0f7343aad018576301fcc4ec441ac1.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b59367e0-e2c1-5b73-8886-7978e62c8aa2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d78785e-c462-55f8-9982-52dc361d2a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{1-y}}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544150} {:answer/id #uuid "3160ccd4-b4fb-549a-a695-6ba7523ae759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544196} {:answer/id #uuid "bce3792a-58d1-5db4-971f-0dd4799c18a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-y}}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544489} {:answer/id #uuid "d96c9ebe-385a-5aa9-8cce-0af1acc3fe17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544554} {:answer/id #uuid "e027944a-9ba9-531c-961b-bd1358b11e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544554} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad3fdbb4-05c4-5223-8593-ecad3e3ec912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c3ba9c34-ab2d-5d40-bb57-ca2ed5ef7608" :question/math-academy-id "q-82347" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ab/abf9e5dda9e98caac491aa5d94b3e2ae3f08f90d6bd490762adabc1437ac49d9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}-1\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type II regions. \n\n![](images/a4/a45e4b63b1eff23617abcbe33ce522652b20de6c817dfde2d9cdda52d12bb778.png)\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y),$  is \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\sqrt[3]{y+1}.$\n\nAlso, the right boundary of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\sqrt{1-y}.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y+1}\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-y}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b6285502-e482-59e6-94a7-bdde0989b515" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544906}}] :activity/title "Changing the Order of Integration in Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544075}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167717} :activity/id #uuid "1bdab585-5331-596a-b7b4-0a1f2915ca75" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049562 :topic/id #uuid "ffe9e721-b26e-55d0-9e1f-e1d2a56a5a72" :topic/math-academy-id 3179} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168103 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a differential equation of the form \n\n$$\ny ' = f ( a x + b y ),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are real constants. In this case, we can rewrite the equation as a separable differential equation using the substitution $z= ax+by.$ We then proceed to solve this separable equation.\n\n To see how we apply this method, let's consider the following example: \n\n$$\ny' = (x+y)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = g(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = z^2+ 1.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡z & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the general solution to the equation in terms of $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arctan {(x+y)}  = x + C\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f67d9540-b344-56cf-9d27-747a656e8f6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5345 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2d610c8e-cba3-5dd1-81e3-dc49d08dbbe3" :step/math-academy-id 33447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166701 :question/id #uuid "870077e3-99f9-52b9-ac39-26bbc3827466" :question/math-academy-id "e-9035"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483412}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "d30e0cc8-f5fd-57d0-abd3-963f38723c6f" :step/math-academy-id 33752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166702 :question/id #uuid "d3145755-b414-57e5-8039-04ceb1a3a5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9037"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491156}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "c61c2ab1-44eb-5ab7-a043-c8efa2b4445d" :step/math-academy-id 33754}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs by Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166982}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491265} :activity/id #uuid "5396071c-65c8-5ca3-8777-9084be368206" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049562 :topic/id #uuid "ffe9e721-b26e-55d0-9e1f-e1d2a56a5a72" :topic/math-academy-id 3179} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a488a6a-c562-5116-a808-9606ad5fcd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241996} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9114ac-eb6e-5022-a9dc-0dfb399af7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242175} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe35dec-6d42-59f5-8111-b8e850cce8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242858} {:answer/id #uuid "cb622b0b-0e9a-5e4a-8f28-6fb1f1e8c0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242971} {:answer/id #uuid "e106123d-d9ac-599d-b55f-445e9e4d1b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243180} :answer-field/id #uuid "579ac401-6a63-5a61-82b5-87d55aa593a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fe2488e6-1056-5c66-a150-25aaef8df7c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108858" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{(2x+y)\\,{2}^{2x+y}}-2.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={2}^{z} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+C \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+K \\\\ {2}^{z}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=K\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}).$"} :step/id #uuid "087cb510-8904-5d85-852e-0bf683d473d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491269}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491227 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f3aab70-1877-5ee8-a896-a828fe6ad391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{(x+y+2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241454} {:answer/id #uuid "61f69b05-6c80-5dbd-8fb8-f0aead30c675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+y+2)^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242059} {:answer/id #uuid "68e92ed8-70bc-5a3e-a2e3-29f9b53dd4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242122} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3ea3a5-0dd3-5cea-8cf5-a5dedd54b5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(x+y+2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242544} {:answer/id #uuid "e050865e-0cfc-5a7c-8d03-dafcfb932d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242544} :answer-field/id #uuid "077176d7-a950-5c43-bdf4-07d7b8ff6ea4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c93f6fd2-747e-585d-8694-c18e2cf8aef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108840" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+y+2)^{3}-1.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=C-2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =(z+2)^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=(z+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(z+2)^{3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(z+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2(z+2)^{2}} & =x+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{(z+2)^{2}} & =C-2x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(x+y+2)^{2}}=C-2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{(x+y+2)^{2}}.$"} :step/id #uuid "2cc8d661-9c6a-5f74-933a-4805142d45f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491221}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491256 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f80959a-efe4-515b-ab20-fae5251c37ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490570} {:answer/id #uuid "783a347a-bf54-5822-92c5-cd38e0951ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x-y+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490749} {:answer/id #uuid "7a868760-39cb-55eb-9223-ed909c3d82ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490754} {:answer/id #uuid "d3724410-1b69-54fd-82ce-033b21b6c201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490907} {:answer/id #uuid "ecddebaf-9db9-54b0-8d19-3046d28f4b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490570} :answer-field/id #uuid "42278d90-82a6-5f26-9592-9f5a4df7efef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "65c1b757-1dae-5815-b9ce-f664cca855c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-149719" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x-y+2.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =z+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=z.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|z| & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|.$"} :step/id #uuid "a842b3a0-1650-5294-af8c-a8010e523e14"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491265 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e859479-1214-5aa1-9663-6484c58e67d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378410} {:answer/id #uuid "461504be-45af-5511-a6e4-22297a20ac20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378411} {:answer/id #uuid "fe707789-7e96-5e0d-b56c-0e2e3ceb68e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378412} {:answer/id #uuid "9c36d855-eba9-57f3-8ba4-8ac6197d9a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378413} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1f358a-a2f0-5f9b-b0e9-1d77c448acf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378412} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff18d72-ae56-5bb5-b84e-265967baa590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f97d6af-c63f-5608-867b-bbac32e035d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-108807" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-8x).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=3y-8x,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3y-8x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{8}{3} & =3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=1-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.$"} :step/id #uuid "c3d1c306-6a8a-5b40-98d3-f38258a0e7ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491227}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fdf75de-061d-588c-a360-fed5ae1090a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-2\\sqrt[3]{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272935} {:answer/id #uuid "532850a1-3a73-586a-8762-10e055fa15e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[3]{z-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273094} {:answer/id #uuid "6375c28c-5e9e-5f53-8864-e51769fb068f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273243} {:answer/id #uuid "c7aff33e-bce5-51d7-9451-a5e068d2a70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{2z-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274124} {:answer/id #uuid "e2993fb8-03c7-5b2a-9fb0-686528082cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274394}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272935} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7af1607-0564-589d-85f1-119c58380d52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dabceef6-e8d3-5182-b240-ffcea0ff3af5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149710" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt[3]{x-2y}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x-2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt[3]{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-2\\sqrt[3]{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=1-2\\sqrt[3]{z}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c9a7b8cf-5c66-5737-a760-835d1e04663a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491256}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs by Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490502}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153161} :activity/id #uuid "656cb00f-9a01-5d98-b674-14d57dda7e9a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049808 :topic/id #uuid "15638861-fef2-549b-9318-e9b1cdad2ec4" :topic/math-academy-id 2759} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153161 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153408 :tutorial/content "The standard basis of the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb{R}$ with degree at most $2$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2\\}.\n$$\n\nAnother basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ t^2-1, \\: t+3, \\: 2t-1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn general, vector spaces have infinitely many bases. \n\nLet's now consider the following theorem:\n\n*If a basis of a vector space $V$ has $n$ elements, then every basis of $V$ has $n$ elements.*\n\nThe number $n$ is called the **dimension** of the vector space, and we write $\\textrm{dim}(V)=n.$\n\nSo, since the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ has $3$ elements, all of its bases have $3$ elements, and we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_2[t])=3.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "91a87b01-a3a9-5fd9-865d-2e7fa9fb4636" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4971 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1c281721-48ee-5e84-901c-07d72bf77044" :step/math-academy-id 653421 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153162}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153162 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152029 :question/id #uuid "2bcc7bb9-1b5e-5858-811d-1e5c14f0bc29" :question/math-academy-id "e-8288"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484265}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"} :step/id #uuid "977a3893-c3c0-5448-ab25-3271a4a3a0ff" :step/math-academy-id 653422 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153166}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153163 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152030 :question/id #uuid "e1ae146f-04ae-5662-9ea3-178a23b3c366" :question/math-academy-id "e-8289"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495107}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "32c06dcf-98e2-59eb-83b8-eaa3a8573b55" :step/math-academy-id 653428 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153164}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153164 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153409 :tutorial/content "When a vector space $V$ has a finite dimension $\\textrm{dim}(V) = n < \\infty,$ the vector space is **finite-dimensional**.\n\nSome vector spaces can have arbitrarily large linearly independent sets. In such cases, the vector space is **infinite-dimensional**.\n\nAn example of an infinite-dimensional vector space is the space of all continuous functions $C(\\mathbb{R})$ defined on $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTo understand why $C(\\mathbb{R})$ is an infinite-dimensional vector space, we note that for any positive integer $n,$ the following set of continuous functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, t^n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^n x_i t^i = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $x_i=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\\ldots,n.$ Since $n$ can be arbitrarily large, our vector space cannot have finite dimension." :tutorial/id #uuid "cdc61d9a-3a5b-5d82-b2f8-17ebc848ac95" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4972 :tutorial/title "Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "17e7956b-85c4-535f-b3e9-ef87f6a27c75" :step/math-academy-id 653431 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153165}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153165 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152031 :question/id #uuid "6d2ba662-f58e-56ec-bf87-c1f26c12c1c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8290"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495165}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "7a4fa43c-f576-546c-b417-c6b36b6e838e" :step/math-academy-id 653432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153166 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152222 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152032 :question/id #uuid "61fa85c6-5b27-5f81-a7ba-363eb68e8d3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8315"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495081}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"} :step/id #uuid "cb8f5e62-9e00-5ea4-b454-29f57b6bd487" :step/math-academy-id 653425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153163}}] :activity/title "Dimension in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152367}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495236} :activity/id #uuid "8fd7c257-aa59-5f20-b67e-9f32e957099a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049808 :topic/id #uuid "15638861-fef2-549b-9318-e9b1cdad2ec4" :topic/math-academy-id 2759} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d9255a3-f808-53ab-9a1d-bce0b2aee8d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494406} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad4f74f-98bf-5d22-9457-443e51eb0e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494468} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d9093c-41de-586e-8b9a-a4e06e3dceda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494702} {:answer/id #uuid "b2867c1c-1f11-5f60-8eeb-04a47f542fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494734} {:answer/id #uuid "c9096bb1-9011-5dc6-aea5-546459a5a73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494790}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494734} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b5c8af9-d959-569a-8058-100af23b2c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "872bbc86-b2d9-5fc0-a340-666aa227cedf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95225" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $12?$\n\nI. ${R}^{12}$\n\nII. ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 4}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 4$ matrices over $R$\n\nIII. ${R}_{11}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $11$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\nI. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}^{12})=12.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3\\times 4}(R))=3\\cdot 4=12.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nIII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}_{11}[t])=11+1=12.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "6ac3edfc-26f8-589b-8f2c-b805c5ecd692" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495231}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495217 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08461b80-8ee5-589b-a179-259194936ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494340} {:answer/id #uuid "5af31435-c11c-5c3f-b06e-2a10c6c1c500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494502} {:answer/id #uuid "771ae68d-4a83-5f4f-9dcc-443975716b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494573} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a39110-5421-5df4-bfb4-13a5169af020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494735} {:answer/id #uuid "c31ee08d-68d0-5e26-b97b-8161c20fe5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494340} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e9553e-a36d-5ce4-9531-7c5e67d94d60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0776bb6-a5c9-57f2-a5dc-9cd00549186b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95233" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${R}^{750}$\n\nII. ${R}^{\\infty },$ the vector space of infinite real sequences\n\nIII. ${\\text{M}}_{50}(R),$ the vector space of all $50\\times 50$ matrices over $R$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${R}^{\\infty }$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of sequences is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}\\},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in \\{1,2,…,n\\},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${R}^{\\infty }$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${R}^{\\infty }$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}^{750}$ and ${\\text{M}}_{50}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{750})=750,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{50}(R))={50}^{2}=2\\,500\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7b787e2b-3bc8-5c8f-b9c3-b74f50bd3683" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495223}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495223 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bd1a089-11bd-5537-b24d-b5320d066943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494404} {:answer/id #uuid "7b41ed65-ebd8-5e8a-9322-4359d606455b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494583} {:answer/id #uuid "98625c99-1d21-5d81-8b45-7668916611fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494665} {:answer/id #uuid "c68081df-1e95-51e2-8875-d1f0093b0cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494784} {:answer/id #uuid "ca864cf8-1d57-54a6-bac6-6cdaa11ca7a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494665} :answer-field/id #uuid "b725a1ed-492c-532e-82ae-acdc74378877" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4868b354-15b9-5c4c-bfd0-122202f40c50" :question/math-academy-id "q-106882" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 5}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 5$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 5}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6\\times 5}(R))=6\\cdot 5=30.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8c095d32-ef2b-570d-930d-b9c7020f763d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495231 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32aca3cf-a5e2-53e0-9465-8c5812ddcb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494418} {:answer/id #uuid "500ce576-4c92-5f17-8883-d14fb5dd6073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494479} {:answer/id #uuid "9317dd78-6bb1-51d8-9527-c7283331155e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494653} {:answer/id #uuid "cab46aea-83c2-537f-b7c2-3ead6ff50e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494797} {:answer/id #uuid "ce013736-d830-5648-bf13-13959d355826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494418} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae053587-50f1-5f8c-b674-29dc44c5f648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "300def0f-69c7-5821-80a9-ead2c4071d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-95228" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $18?$\n\nI. ${R}_{17}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $17$\n\nII. ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 3}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 3$ matrices over $R$\n\nIII. ${R}^{17}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\nI. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}_{17}[t])=17+1=18.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6\\times 3}(R))=6\\cdot 3=18.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nIII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}^{17})=17\\ne 18.\\,\\times \n  $$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9ee124b4-4e89-5c70-ae8c-f8a1677ea468" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495217}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21209143-d6f3-527a-95fc-698f6fe33a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358043} {:answer/id #uuid "5bddb206-b7cb-5ec9-a288-f2949be70a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358550} {:answer/id #uuid "8a3475b2-1900-543b-9144-6a374fa7f5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358956} {:answer/id #uuid "b011e497-97d8-5a66-bc41-ff74a3cf84e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359280} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d5201c-0d42-5c30-b6c4-c732698b84a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359580}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359280} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2fb2713-16be-554b-924a-e6711ddefc4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa93d1d7-d70a-58d2-9a8d-2974756902aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-95221" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{4}(R),$ the vector space of all $4\\times 4$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{4}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4}(R))={4}^{2}=16.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a70eb95a-1d4a-5112-b093-41836097c112" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495209}}] :activity/title "Dimension in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494309}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125298} :activity/id #uuid "00d7af21-acdd-52ae-8ffc-37290b0f0c74" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027 :topic/id #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" :topic/math-academy-id 1939} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125735 :tutorial/content "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$ *\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental  link between\ncalculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)=  x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$  It is easy to see that  $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ \n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$ *\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$  is parallel to the level curve.\n\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot  \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68a96106-e224-5989-af04-8d464b6e58d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2499 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35b375f8-a01b-56db-97b7-ac88f2a7883a" :step/math-academy-id 11200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125306}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124265 :question/id #uuid "b8884af1-186f-582b-8de2-a137a66649b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3432"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385508}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"} :step/id #uuid "74d5fcb5-d32b-5ddd-92cd-491ff00e4b54" :step/math-academy-id 11376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124266 :question/id #uuid "3ab5ece1-03aa-5fb0-9952-b858b6ca67b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3433"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537133} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544815}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "147b9868-60b3-5d25-8663-966137fb6540" :step/math-academy-id 11379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125740 :tutorial/content "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if  the  gradient vector is nonzero, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$ \n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\[5pt]\n&= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\[10pt]\n\n\\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle.\n}\n$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "86223826-30df-5eba-9375-355401199095" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2524 :tutorial/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector For Functions of More than Two Variables"} :step/id #uuid "81eef922-1c17-502c-89a2-3e132f4a64e6" :step/math-academy-id 11383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124267 :question/id #uuid "d3222782-abc1-5cd3-bceb-27e0585f9e09" :question/math-academy-id "e-3434"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527163}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"} :step/id #uuid "20b0d4d3-7059-5c01-9a72-d14629d41a1c" :step/math-academy-id 11384 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124268 :question/id #uuid "db58088e-59e0-5ca4-82a2-991bb6a4e7be" :question/math-academy-id "e-3435"} :knowledge-point/id #uui